      "content": "Yes, you are hitting on a critical distinction where framing often shifts from what the protesters *want* to what they *do*. When protests become intense, especially those involving minority groups, descriptors like \"unrest,\" \"clashes,\" or \"rioting\" are more likely to appear, even if only a minority of participants are engaged in aggressive behavior. Media outlets often conflate the protest's underlying cause with the most visible moments of disorder to create a narrative of chaos. This highlights how the framing choice dictates whether the focus remains on the social issue or devolves into a description of public disruption.",
